<h2>The rule and its proviso</h2>
<p>Order VI Rule 17 CPC embodies two opposing policies. The main rule is liberal: the court may at any stage allow either party to alter or amend pleadings in such manner as may be just, and all amendments necessary for determining the real questions in controversy shall be made. The proviso, inserted in 2002 to curb dilatory amendments, is strict: once the trial has commenced, no amendment shall be allowed unless the court concludes that, in spite of due diligence, the party could not have raised the matter before. Post-2002 jurisprudence has largely been an exercise in locating individual cases on the line between the two.</p>

<h2>How the dispute travelled</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>2009</strong> — Rabindranath Sahoo sues in the court of the Senior Civil Judge, Cuttack (C.S. No. 86 of 2009) for declaration of title, recovery of possession and rectification of two sale deeds.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>During trial</strong> — after defendants disclose their plot records, the plaintiff searches the Tahsildar\'s office and finds a compromise decree in T.S. No. 105 of 1998 touching a portion of the suit property, passed in a suit to which he was never a party; certified copies are obtained on 22 June 2020.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>2022–2023</strong> — the trial court rejects the amendment application; the High Court declines to interfere. The plaintiff dies, and his heirs pursue the matter.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>12 October 2026</strong> — the Supreme Court sets aside both orders and allows the amendment on payment of Rs. 25,000 costs, with consequential amendments within four weeks and liberty to the defendants to file additional written statements.</div>
</div>

<h2>What satisfied the due-diligence test</h2>
<p>The Court accepted the explanation that a stranger to a decree cannot be presumed to know of it. The plaintiff\'s knowledge began with the defendants\' own disclosures, was completed by the certified copies, and the application followed. Due diligence, in other words, is tested against what the party could realistically have discovered — not against an omniscient standard. Equally important was what the amendment did not do: it introduced no new cause of action foreign to the suit. The decree concerned the same property; the declaration sought was ancillary to the title and possession questions already on trial. An amendment that merely enlarges the grounds on which the existing controversy is fought stands on a different footing from one that substitutes a new case.</p>

<h2>Prejudice, costs and the alternative of multiplicity</h2>
<table class="law">
<tr><th>Factor</th><th>How the Court weighed it</th></tr>
<tr><td>Stage of the suit</td><td>Trial had commenced, so the proviso applied — but the explanation for late discovery satisfied it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nature of the suit</td><td>Unchanged: same property, overlapping title and possession issues</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prejudice to defendants</td><td>Curable by additional written statements and costs of Rs. 25,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Limitation</td><td>Arguable, since the date of knowledge was disputed — left open for trial</td></tr>
<tr><td>Refusal\'s consequence</td><td>Would force a separate suit over the same title — multiplicity the amendment avoids</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Why the case matters in Delhi practice</h2>
<p>Suits over family property in the District Courts of Delhi routinely surface old decrees, settlement deeds and mutation orders mid-trial, because revenue and registration records are searched seriously only once litigation hardens. This judgment confirms that discovery of such a document during trial is not fatal to pleading it — provided the party moves promptly, documents the trail of discovery, and the new plea genuinely belongs to the existing controversy. It also reiterates the other side of the coin: questions of limitation and the special bar of Order XXIII Rule 3A (which requires a compromise decree to be challenged in the same suit in which it was passed) remain fully alive for trial, so an allowed amendment is the beginning of an argument, not its end.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>Neutral citation 2026 INSC 1112; the judgment is reportable. The appeal arose from SLP (C) No. 25635 of 2023 against orders of the Orissa High Court and the trial court at Cuttack.</p>
</div>
